<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android blocks plain HTTP from API 28 onwards.

  This file cannot express "private addresses only". A <domain> entry matches an
  exact host - there is no CIDR, no wildcard - so "192.168.0.0" means that one
  literal address and nothing else. An earlier version of this file listed a few
  such addresses believing they covered the range; they did not, and the app
  refused to reach a server on 192.168.88.7.

  So cleartext is permitted here, and the judgement about whether that is safe
  moves to Config.isCleartextToNetwork, which can actually tell loopback from a
  private address from a public one. The app warns on its first screen when the
  token would cross a network in the clear, and warns far more loudly when the
  address is public.

  The real fix is HTTPS, and the cheapest route is a tunnel - the same
  Cloudflare Tunnel arrangement EquiStar already uses, which gives a hostname
  and a certificate without owning a domain or opening a port. Behind one, point
  the app at the https:// URL and this file can be deleted along with
  android:networkSecurityConfig in the manifest.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
